  "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x)-{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x) & =1 \\\\ 1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x) & ={cosh}^{2}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2(1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x))}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
